# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# @sip/worker — every variable the process reads, and why.
#
#   cp packages/worker/.env.example packages/worker/.env
#
# A dry run needs the chain block and nothing else. The live block is read ONLY
# when SIP_WORKER_ALLOW_BROADCAST matches its sentence byte for byte, so a
# half-filled file is a dry run, never a surprise pull.
#
# THE DEPLOYMENT ADDRESSES ARE BLANK ON PURPOSE, AND THE WORKER REFUSES TO START
# WITHOUT THEM.
#
# SIP has no deployment yet, and the forked project's factory and executor are
# abandoned rather than inherited. Their trading accounts carry a savings rate
# that meant a PERCENTAGE OF PROFIT — 1000 to 3000 bps, read from chain on
# 2026-09-08 — while this worker attests VOLUME, and Phase 0 carries that volume
# in the attestation's cash fields. Pointing at one of those accounts would apply
# a profit-era rate to a notional and skim about a hundred times what the user
# agreed to, out of somebody else's wallet. That is why there is no default and
# no fallback anywhere in the code: deploy SIP's own contracts and set these.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── chain: required in every mode ────────────────────────────────────────────

# One or more http(s) JSON-RPC endpoints for chain 4663, comma-separated,
# preferred first — the failover walks the list in order and re-probes the
# primary once it recovers. SIP_RPC_URL is accepted as a single-entry alias.
#
# THIS MUST BE AN ALCHEMY PAY-AS-YOU-GO ENDPOINT, AND IT MUST BE ARCHIVE.
# Two separate requirements, both measured rather than assumed:
#
#   * ARCHIVE. The reconciler reads eth_getBalance at N−1 and at N for blocks far
#     older than the 128 a full node keeps, so a pruning endpoint cannot answer
#     the question the whole reconciliation is built on. Pay-As-You-Go on 4663 is
#     archive — confirmed against the chain, not read off a pricing page.
#   * NOT THE FREE TIER. It caps eth_getLogs at a 10-BLOCK range, which makes
#     discovery impossible: the factory scan and the Transfer sweep each cover
#     thousands of blocks per tick, and no amount of chunking turns that into a
#     workable number of requests.
#
# Assume this URL carries an API key. It is registered with the redactor the
# moment it is read, so no log line, warning or refusal can print it.
SIP_RPC_URLS=

# VaultFactory. Trading wallets are discovered from its TradingAccountLinked and
# TradingAccountUnlinked logs, with activeVaultOf() as the authority. Blank on
# purpose — see the header.
SIP_VAULT_FACTORY=

# Where the pull is sent: the deployed SettlementExecutor in Phase 0,
# SipVolumeExecutor in Phase 1. Blank on purpose — see the header.
#
# ONE THING TO REMEMBER WHEN THIS CHANGES: the Privy policy that bounds the
# worker's seat pins the executor's address, so a new deployment needs that
# policy updated to the new address before any live pull can be signed. Until it
# is, Privy refuses the signature and the window simply stays open.
SIP_SETTLEMENT_EXECUTOR=

# The first L2 block worth scanning — the block the factory above was deployed
# at. Required rather than defaulted to 0, which would rescan from genesis on
# every tick and find nothing for most of it.
SIP_LOGS_FROM_BLOCK=

# Chain id. Defaults to 4663; set it only to assert which chain you mean.
SIP_CHAIN_ID=4663

# ── storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres for the ledger. Optional in dry run, where a memory ledger stands in;
# REQUIRED in live mode, because that memory ledger forgets every INTENT and
# SENT pull on restart and a forgotten pull is a window pulled twice.
#
# Use the SESSION pooler on port 5432. Port 6543 is the transaction pooler,
# which keeps no session, and the advisory lock that stops two workers running
# at once is session scoped — it would appear to be granted and hold nothing.
# The worker refuses that port by name rather than discovering it as a duplicate
# pull. SIP_DATABASE_URL is accepted as an alias.
DATABASE_URL=

# ── live mode only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable in this block is removed from the environment in EVERY mode,
# and read only when the sentinel below matches. In dry run they are deleted by
# name, unread: the process cannot sign a pull because it holds nothing that
# could sign one.

# The one string that arms the worker, compared byte for byte:
#
#   i-understand-this-moves-real-funds
#
# Anything else — 1, true, the right sentence with a trailing newline or a
# different case — stays in dry run and says so in a warning.
SIP_WORKER_ALLOW_BROADCAST=

# The key registered in AttesterRegistry. It signs attestations of volume and
# holds no funds; it is not a wallet key, and pasting a wallet key here is the
# one mistake the config refuses with a sentence telling you to rotate it.
SIP_ATTESTER_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Privy. The pull is signed by the trading wallet itself, through the app's
# authorization key seated on that wallet under a policy — the worker never
# holds a trading key. All three are required together in live mode: a seat
# without its key cannot sign, and a key without its app cannot be presented.
# PRIVY_AUTHORIZATION_KEY is accepted as an alias of the third.
PRIVY_APP_ID=
PRIVY_APP_SECRET=
PRIVY_AUTHORIZATION_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ── tuning: all optional, and the defaults are the tested ones ───────────────

SIP_POLL_MS=300000          # one tick every five minutes
SIP_FINALITY_MARGIN_L2=64   # a window closes this many L2 blocks behind the head
SIP_MAX_LOG_SPAN=10000      # eth_getLogs chunk size; 0 scans nothing, forever

# ── read, but supplied by the host rather than by you ────────────────────────
# RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_ID and RAILWAY_REPLICA_ID (or SIP_WORKER_DEPLOYMENT_ID and
# SIP_WORKER_REPLICA_ID) are read for one purpose: they go into the Postgres
# application_name, so whoever finds a worker holding the advisory lock can tell
# which container it is and whether it is the live one or a stale dry run.
